{
 'title': 'Reading the Maya glyphs', 'context': 'A lecture in an archaeology course on how the Maya writing system was deciphered.',
 'vocab': ['glyph', 'logogram', 'syllable', 'codex', 'inscription'],
 'script': [
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Good morning. Today we're looking at one of the great intellectual achievements of the twentieth century: the decipherment of Maya writing. The Maya of southern Mexico and Central America developed the most complete writing system in the ancient Americas, one that could record anything their language could express. It was in use from at least the third century BC until after the Spanish conquest in the sixteenth century."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Most surviving Maya writing is carved in stone, on monuments, temple stairways and altars, but the Maya also painted texts on pottery and wrote books. These books, which we call codices, were made from bark, which was pounded into sheets, coated with a fine layer of lime plaster, and folded like a screen. Thousands must once have existed. Only four survive today, and the best known is the Dresden Codex, named after the German city where it's kept. It contains remarkably accurate astronomical tables, including calculations of the movements of the planet Venus and predictions of eclipses."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Why did so few books survive? Partly because bark paper decays quickly in a tropical climate. But there was also deliberate destruction. In 1562, a Spanish friar named Diego de Landa, who later became Bishop of Yucatán, ordered the burning of a large number of Maya books at the town of Maní, because he regarded them as works of the devil. The great irony is that Landa also wrote an account of Maya life, which contains what he believed to be the Maya alphabet. This document would eventually become a key to the decipherment, although it lay forgotten in a Spanish library until the nineteenth century."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "The problem with Landa's alphabet was that it didn't seem to work. For decades, scholars tried to read texts with it and failed. The reason, we now know, is that the Maya script is not an alphabet. It is what we call a logosyllabic system. Some signs, called logograms, represent whole words, while others represent syllables, usually a consonant followed by a vowel. When Landa asked his informant to write the Spanish letters, the scribe wrote the Maya syllable that sounded most like the name of each letter."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "The breakthrough came from an unexpected direction. In 1952, a young Soviet scholar, Yuri Knorozov, published an article arguing that Landa's signs should be read as syllables. Knorozov never visited a Maya site until very late in his life; he worked from published drawings in Leningrad. His ideas were rejected for many years, especially by the British scholar Eric Thompson, who was the leading authority in the field and believed the glyphs were mainly symbolic rather than phonetic."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "The second great step was taken by Tatiana Proskouriakoff, a Russian-born American who had trained as an architect. In 1960, she studied the dates on monuments at the site of Piedras Negras and noticed that they fell into groups, each covering a period no longer than a human lifetime. She concluded that the inscriptions recorded the births, accessions and deaths of real rulers. Until then, most scholars had believed the texts dealt only with the calendar, astronomy and religion. Suddenly, the Maya had a political history, with named kings and queens, wars and alliances."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "From the 1970s onwards, progress was rapid. At a famous conference held at Palenque in 1973, a small group of researchers worked out the sequence of the city's rulers in a single afternoon, including its most famous king, Pakal. Today, specialists can read the great majority of known Maya inscriptions, although some signs are still uncertain."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Before we finish, I want to mention numbers. The Maya counted in base twenty rather than base ten, and they wrote numbers using just three symbols: a dot for one, a bar for five, and a shell to represent zero. That makes them one of the very few ancient peoples to have developed the concept of zero independently. And finally, it's worth remembering that the Maya did not disappear. Millions of people speak Maya languages today, and some are now learning to read and write the script of their ancestors. Next week, we'll look at the Maya calendar in more detail."),
 ],
 'groups': [
  G('Questions 31–40. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.', [
   Q('note', 'Maya codices: made from __________, coated with lime plaster and folded', 'bark', 'were made from bark', 'The books were made from bark.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Dresden Codex: tables showing movements of the planet __________', 'Venus', 'calculations of the movements of the planet Venus', 'It tracks Venus.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Diego de Landa ordered the __________ of many Maya books at Maní in 1562', 'burning', 'ordered the burning of a large number of Maya books at the town of Maní', 'He had them burned.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Landa recorded what he thought was the Maya __________', 'alphabet', 'contains what he believed to be the Maya alphabet', 'He recorded an "alphabet".', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Some signs (logograms) stand for whole words; others stand for __________', 'syllables', 'while others represent syllables', 'Other signs represent syllables.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Knorozov worked from published __________ rather than visiting sites', 'drawings', 'he worked from published drawings in Leningrad', 'He used published drawings.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Thompson believed glyphs were mainly __________, not phonetic', 'symbolic', 'believed the glyphs were mainly symbolic rather than phonetic', 'He thought they were symbolic.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Proskouriakoff showed that inscriptions recorded the lives of real __________', 'rulers', 'the births, accessions and deaths of real rulers', 'They recorded rulers\' lives.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'In 1973, at __________, researchers worked out a list of a city\'s kings', 'Palenque', 'At a famous conference held at Palenque in 1973', 'The conference was at Palenque.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Maya numbers: a __________ was used to represent zero', 'shell', 'a shell to represent zero', 'A shell meant zero.', max=1),
  ]),
 ],
},
